The user is reporting that the folder isn't being created, and even if they manually create the folder, the form doesn't get moved into it. Let's think about why this might fail.

Given the pattern we've seen, this likely throws an exception that's being silently caught by our `try { ... } catch (e) { /* 移動に失敗しても、フォーム自体は作成済みなので処理は続行する */ }` block. Since we swallow the error silently, the user doesn't see any error message about this step failing - the form just doesn't move, no error shown. This matches what's reported: "フォルダも作られないし...フォルダの中に入らない" with no error visible (otherwise they'd have mentioned an error message).

So the very first thing to do: stop silently swallowing this error, and instead surface it (similar to how we did with the "step" diagnostic pattern) so we can see what's actually happening. Let me modify the catch block to log/report the failure instead of just silently ignoring it. Actually, since the overall function already has the outer try-catch wrapping with `step` that converts to a thrown Error with the step name + message — but the inner try-catch for folder-move SWALLOWS the exception (catches and does nothing), so it never reaches the outer catch. I need to either remove the inner try-catch (let it propagate to outer catch and surface as an error) or, better, catch it but attach the error info to the result object so the user can see "moved" or "failed to move: <reason>" without breaking the entire form creation.

I think the best approach: don't let folder-move failure abort the whole process (since the form is already created and that's the primary value), but DO surface the failure reason somewhere visible — e.g., include a `folderMoveError` field in the returned result object, and have the client UI display a note like "フォルダへの移動に失�exceptionsした：(reason)" if present. This way: form creation still succeeds even if folder move fails, but the reason is visible for debugging, consistent with the "step" diagnostic pattern they've found helpful throughout this conversation.

Now, let's think about WHY the move might be failing. Potential causes:
1. `DriveApp.getFoldersByName(FOLDER_NAME)` / `DriveApp.createFolder(FOLDER_NAME)` might throw "this operation is not supported" similar to other Forms-related quirks — unlikely, DriveApp folder creation is a very standard, well-supported operation. Should work fine for any account type.
2. `DriveApp.getFileById(form.getId())` - this should also work fine, it's just wrapping the form's underlying Drive file.
3. `.moveTo(folder)` - this is the new-ish method. Let me reconsider: does `moveTo` require the `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive` scope (full Drive scope) rather than just the narrower `drive.file` scope that might be auto-detected from `DriveApp.getFileById` usage? Since this is the first time we're calling DriveApp methods in this script (FormApp was the main scope before, plus UrlFetchApp for oEmbed, plus PropertiesService) — adding DriveApp usage introduces a NEW OAuth scope requirement that the user (and any other circle members) has NOT yet consented to! 

This is very likely the actual root cause: **Apps Script automatically detects required scopes from the code, but the existing deployment's authorization was granted BEFORE this DriveApp code was added. Since scope changes require re-authorization, the script might be silently failing because the necessary Drive permission was never granted by the user.**

Actually, more specifically: for Execute as "User accessing the web app", each user authorizes scopes via the OAuth consent screen on first use. If we ADD a new scope requirement (Drive access) to the code after they already authorized once, Apps Script needs the user to RE-AUTHORIZE for the new scope to take effect. Until they do, calls requiring the new scope would fail with a permission/authorization error - but would this error be "operation not supported" or would it be a different, more specific permission error message? Typically, missing scope authorization in Apps Script raises something like "You do not have permission to call DriveApp.getFoldersByName" or similar explicit permission errors, not "not supported" — but our code currently swallows this in the silent try-catch, so the user never sees ANY error message regardless of what specific exception text is thrown. That's the real bug — we need to stop hiding it.

Given the user reports NO error at all (just silently not happening), and "フォームの作成" (the overall flow) apparently still completing successfully apparently (they didn't mention getting stuck or seeing the success screen fail) — this is fully consistent with our silent catch block hiding the actual error.

So my fix: 
1. Stop swallowing the folder-move error silently; capture the message and either include it in console.log (Apps Script Logger/console.log, visible in Executions log) and/or, more usefully, return it back to the client as a non-fatal note so they can see it directly in the UI (consistent with what's worked well throughout this conversation).

Let's modify code:

```js
let folderMoveError = null;
step = 'フォルダへの移動';
try {
  const folder = getOrCreateNetamiseFolder_();
  DriveApp.getFileById(form.getId()).moveTo(folder);
} catch (e) {
  folderMoveError = (e && e.message) ? e.message : String(e);
}

step = 'URLの取得';
const result = {
  editUrl: form.getEditUrl(),
  viewUrl: form.getPublishedUrl(),
  title: title,
  folderMoveError: folderMoveError
};
```

Then on the client side, after success, if `res.folderMoveError` is truthy, show a small warning note: "フォルダへの移動はできませんでした：" + res.folderMoveError, alongside the success links. This way, form creation still succeeds (primary goal preserved), but now they'd see the actual reason for the folder-move failure, which is exactly the missing piece needed to diagnose this.

Also — I should reconsider: maybe the real fix is more direct. Since this is very likely a missing-scope/re-authorization issue, I should also proactively explain to the user that adding Drive folder functionality requires them to **re-authorize** the script (since new scopes were added), and that the standard way for this to happen in Apps Script Web Apps with "Execute as: User accessing the web app" is: when the deployed code's required scopes change, returning users might keep using a CACHED authorization that doesn't include the new scope, OR Apps Script might force a new consent screen automatically the next time they open the URL since manifest scopes changed. Actually I recall: Apps Script automatically detects scope changes in code, and when you deploy a NEW VERSION with different required scopes, users who already authorized the OLD version's scope set will be prompted again to authorize the NEW scope set the next time they trigger a function requiring it — this should happen automatically without extra effort, showing a new "needs additional permission" prompt. If this consent prompt is somehow being silently blocked or not appearing (e.g., in an iframe context within google.script.run calls, since you can't show a full OAuth consent popup easily inside an XHR-style call — actually, google.script.run calls that require additional unauthorized scopes typically cause Apps Script to surface a special "authorization needed" response that the HtmlService client recognizes and shows a built-in "authorization needed" dialog/popup automatically). Hmm, this might not work smoothly within our custom UI if not handled specially, but typically Apps Script handles this natively for web apps (shows a popup "this app needs additional authorization, click here to authorize").

Given the complexity, the most robust+simple fix remains: surface the actual error message back to the user via our existing diagnostic pattern (non-fatal warning attached to the result), so we get concrete information rather than guessing further. Let's implement this.

